     private:
 
+    // The members are laid out so that everything the library reads or writes is in
+    // the first 96 bytes of a Parcel in a 64-bit process and the first 52 in a 32-bit
+    // one, while the Parcel itself stays the size upstream gives it, 120 and 60.
+    //
+    // Code that keeps a Parcel on its stack reserves the size its own header said.
+    // Up to Android 9 that was 104 and 52 bytes, and the constructor of today's
+    // Parcel, as upstream lays it out, writes past them. Two of the vendor files of
+    // 2017 on the ASUS Z500KL are caught by that: Qualcomm's peripheral manager
+    // client (libperipheral_client.so, 64-bit), where the write lands on the stack
+    // protector and pm-proxy aborts every five seconds, and the camera HAL
+    // (camera.msm8952.so, 32-bit), in two functions that call the display service.
+    // With this layout both kinds of caller are served: the old ones reserve at least
+    // what is used, the ones compiled against this header reserve what they always did.
+    // Nothing outside libbinder reads the members, so their order concerns nobody else.
+    //
+    // The six flags take the four bytes after mError: three as plain bool (mHasFds
+    // has its address taken) and three as one-bit fields in the fourth byte. What was
+    // mReserved is part of mUnused, the tail, which is never read or written.
     status_t            mError;
+    mutable bool        mHasFds;
+    bool                mAllowFds;
